<p class="banner-question">
  Can we trust AI reasoning &mdash; and how do we establish that trust &mdash; as systems move from
  <em>answering</em> to <em>acting</em>?
</p>

<p>
  That is the question my research program is organized around. When a model produces a chain of
  &ldquo;thought,&rdquo; is that reasoning faithful to what actually drove the answer, or is it a
  performance? Can it be bent by an adversary &mdash; and can it bend us? Can its conclusions be
  challenged and defended, the way we demand of human reasoning in any serious institution? I don't
  speculate about these questions; I build the experiments that answer them.
</p>

<p>
  I evaluate AI reasoning along four properties, at every level from single model to agent to
  multi-agent system, using adversarial probes as the primary instrument.
</p>

<div class="research-grid">
  <div class="research-property">
    <h3>Legibility &amp; faithfulness <span class="property-verb">— can you inspect it?</span></h3>
    <p>
      Reasoning should be open-able, not opaque &mdash; and a model's stated reasoning should be
      faithful to its actual inference process. I approach this with structured and vector-symbolic
      representations (see <a href="https://vsarathy.com/vsax/">VSAX</a>), neuro-symbolic
      argumentation (<a href="https://advancesincognitivesystems.github.io/acs2022/data/acs22_paper-7038.pdf">ACS 2022</a>),
      and my current work at Leidos on agentic verification &mdash; decomposing LLM outputs into claims,
      evidence, assumptions, and uncertainty.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="research-property">
    <h3>Manipulation robustness <span class="property-verb">— does it survive attack?</span></h3>
    <p>
      Two opposite arrows, one theme: can an adversary bend the model's reasoning, and is the model
      bending its user's? This is cognitive security
      (<a href="https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI-SS/article/view/42940">CogSLLaM, AAAI-SS 2026</a>),
      and it's where my legal training does the most work &mdash; law is a decade-long apprenticeship in
      how arguments mislead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="research-property">
    <h3>Contestability <span class="property-verb">— can you challenge it?</span></h3>
    <p>
      A trustworthy conclusion is one that can be argued with: challenged, defended, and revised.
      I build computational argumentation systems
      (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.04528">IntelliProof, AAAI 2026</a>;
      <a href="https://vsarathy.com/arglib/">ArgLib</a>) and benchmarks that test whether models can
      genuinely engage with arguments rather than merely produce them &mdash; including evidence that
      generating arguments for both sides unlocks reasoning a model otherwise can't access
      (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.12997">EMNLP-W 2024</a>).
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="research-property">
    <h3>Social reasoning <span class="property-verb">— does it model other minds?</span></h3>
    <p>
      Systems that act among people must reason about what others believe, intend, and expect &mdash;
      including norms and conversational timing
      (<a href="https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.909/">EMNLP 2024</a>;
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02975">AAAI 2026</a>). Theory-of-mind evaluation is where
      reasoning meets the social world it operates in.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>From models to agents to institutions</h3>

<p>
  Each of these properties gets harder &mdash; and higher-stakes &mdash; as systems move from answering
  questions to taking actions. My earlier work in cognitive robotics and reinforcement learning studied
  reliable action under uncertainty and novelty
  (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13037">SPOTTER, AAMAS 2021</a>;
  <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.12493">RAPid-Learn, ICDL 2022</a>); my current work carries those
  questions to LLM agents &mdash; uncertainty quantification for tool-using systems
  (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.16113">AAMAS 2025</a>) and verification of agentic reasoning.
  The direction I find most compelling is multi-agent: a system of AI agents is an institution, with
  delegation, trust boundaries, and conflicts of interest &mdash; problems law has been engineering
  around for centuries.
</p>

<h3>The method: adversarial evaluation</h3>

<p>
  The instrument behind all of this is the <em>probe</em> &mdash; a targeted manipulation or measurement
  that forces a system to reveal what it won't volunteer. Interventions beat observations; a hostile
  test beats a friendly one. Uncertainty quantification and mechanistic interpretability are probe
  families; benchmarks are probes at scale. Some of this tooling is public on the
  <a href="/tools/">Tools</a> page, and experiments show up on the
  <a href="/blog/">blog</a>.
</p>

